The First National Bank played
a significant role

00:13:26.706 --> 00:13:29.034
in its home city's development.

00:13:30.860 --> 00:13:34.180
-Bank vault, right? Is that what this was?
-That's the bank vault.

00:13:34.247 --> 00:13:36.780
All right. That is cool. Look at that.

00:13:38.046 --> 00:13:41.421
The Flintstone's money
would go in here and a lot of it.

00:13:41.488 --> 00:13:44.407
'Cause they seemed to
have it back in the day.

00:13:44.474 --> 00:13:48.348
And they've turned this into
a beautiful restaurant hotel.

00:13:48.415 --> 00:13:51.697
And we're way early,
so that's why there's nobody here.

00:13:51.764 --> 00:13:54.324
-Is it Genesee or Citizens Bank?
-Genesee Bank.

00:13:54.391 --> 00:13:56.031
-Genesee Bank back in the day?

00:13:56.098 --> 00:13:59.203
It opened up in 1878,
but it was across the street.

00:13:59.270 --> 00:14:01.251
-Okay, so it was
the lumber money at that time

00:14:01.318 --> 00:14:03.709
coming here
before the auto money I believe.

00:14:03.776 --> 00:14:04.609
Oh, yeah.

00:14:04.677 --> 00:14:07.536
The deposits would all go in
behind that door over there.

00:14:07.603 --> 00:14:09.468
The man used to look out at the bank.

00:14:09.535 --> 00:14:11.822
-Oh, right up there?
-For the funny business.

00:14:11.889 --> 00:14:13.929
-Looking for the funny business.

00:14:13.996 --> 00:14:16.841
Growing up here,
this was the greatest place to grow up.

00:14:16.908 --> 00:14:17.741
Yep.

00:14:17.808 --> 00:14:20.713
-Okay, you caught the tail end
of all its prosperity

00:14:20.780 --> 00:14:22.447
when you were growing up?

00:14:23.062 --> 00:14:25.173
Let's see. When did things start changing?

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:26.994
Probably in the mid-'80s.

00:14:27.061 --> 00:14:29.519
-Yeah.
-When General Motors started moving out.

00:14:29.586 --> 00:14:30.786
Moving down south?

00:14:31.117 --> 00:14:36.390
The the crack epidemic hit
in the early to mid-80s.

00:14:36.457 --> 00:14:38.278
Things got a little bad for a while.

00:14:38.345 --> 00:14:42.936
-So GM leaves, the jobs leave,
things go downhill, the drugs come in.

00:14:43.003 --> 00:14:45.870
White flight,
folks move out to the suburbs.

00:14:46.514 --> 00:14:50.091
I just watched a video,
the exact same thing happened in Detroit.

00:14:50.158 --> 00:14:52.287
They had a place called Bottom.

00:14:52.354 --> 00:14:53.295
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:14:53.367 --> 00:14:57.346
They had Paradise City, and then
they cleared it out for a freeway.

00:14:57.413 --> 00:14:58.463
I just watched it.

00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:01.432
They showed the man where
they named the county after him.

00:15:01.499 --> 00:15:04.432
Even with 475,
the highway that came through here

00:15:04.499 --> 00:15:06.087
tore up a lot of communities like…

00:15:06.154 --> 00:15:07.786
-St. John's.
-Okay.

00:15:08.580 --> 00:15:11.848
-So you saw at the tail end of that
at its worst point

00:15:11.915 --> 00:15:13.937
and now it's coming back a bit?

00:15:14.004 --> 00:15:15.498
I think it is. Yeah.

00:15:15.565 --> 00:15:17.385
I mean, I think

00:15:19.110 --> 00:15:20.391
it still has its issues.

00:15:20.458 --> 00:15:22.614
The water crisis didn't help.

00:15:23.333 --> 00:15:24.600
-Is that fixed now?

00:15:25.375 --> 00:15:28.650
-Oh, yeah.
-You guys are good on that front?

00:15:28.717 --> 00:15:32.987
They just announced that
they have replaced the last water line

00:15:33.054 --> 00:15:34.506
that needed to be replaced.

00:15:34.573 --> 00:15:36.477
-Yeah.
-It's all been done.

00:15:36.544 --> 00:15:42.384
I read 11,000 lines, $100 million,
something crazy like that.

00:15:43.224 --> 00:15:44.892
So everyone gets clean water now?

00:15:44.959 --> 00:15:47.622
Yeah. I feel safe drinking the water.

00:15:47.689 --> 00:15:49.416
-Okay.
-And using the water.

00:15:49.483 --> 00:15:51.728
-That wasn't fun though,
back in the day, huh?

00:15:51.795 --> 00:15:53.493
No, it was not.

00:15:53.560 --> 00:15:56.360
We didn't know who to trust,
what to trust.

00:15:57.234 --> 00:16:00.948
We still have people who say,
"I don't want to drink the water."

00:16:01.015 --> 00:16:03.486
Yeah, trust takes time to get back.

00:16:03.553 --> 00:16:05.044
Yeah. It takes time.

00:16:10.937 --> 00:16:14.590
They're taking this old Art Deco building
and putting new windows in.

00:16:14.657 --> 00:16:16.827
So maybe apartments

00:16:17.376 --> 00:16:20.533
and these storefronts,
a lot are empty, some are open.

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:22.385
-How you doing, sir?
-How you doing?

00:16:22.452 --> 00:16:24.333
-They doing apartments here?
What are they doing?

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:28.107
It's just an empty building, man.
They're not trying to rent it or nothing.

00:16:28.174 --> 00:16:29.411
-You from Flint?
-Yeah.

00:16:29.478 --> 00:16:31.064
-How's it going?
-Okay.

00:16:31.131 --> 00:16:33.212
-Going okay? You doing all right?

00:16:33.279 --> 00:16:37.385
I've been homeless a couple of months.
My dad passed away June 11th.

00:16:37.452 --> 00:16:39.936
-I'm sorry, man.
-I've been out here for a while now.

00:16:40.003 --> 00:16:41.568
-Is there nowhere for you to go?

00:16:41.635 --> 00:16:44.203
-No, everything's full right now.
-It's full?

00:16:44.270 --> 00:16:46.662
A lot of homeless people right now?

00:16:46.729 --> 00:16:48.161
I'm sorry about your father.

00:16:48.228 --> 00:16:49.089
Yeah, it sucked.

00:16:49.156 --> 00:16:52.461
He came home from the hospital
and he was gone in his wheelchair.

00:16:52.528 --> 00:16:54.622
Wow. So where are you staying now?

00:16:55.106 --> 00:16:57.983
I sleep on the concrete up at the church.

00:16:58.050 --> 00:16:59.778
GHS is trying to work with me.

00:16:59.845 --> 00:17:02.031
-Who's GHS?
-Genesee Health System.

00:17:02.731 --> 00:17:06.230
Okay. So someone's trying to help you.
That's cool.

00:17:06.297 --> 00:17:07.272
-All the best, sir.

00:17:07.339 --> 00:17:09.339
-All right. Thanks.
-Take care.

00:17:16.161 --> 00:17:19.873
All right, guys. We're going to go
north of the river here in Flint

00:17:19.940 --> 00:17:23.348
where I was told is the roughest hood.

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:25.779
So some quick numbers for you.

00:17:25.846 --> 00:17:28.313
The median household income is $38,000.

00:17:28.978 --> 00:17:32.044
Across the US it's $81,000.

00:17:32.111 --> 00:17:36.898
One third of the population
lives below the poverty line,

00:17:37.847 --> 00:17:41.950
and the murder numbers are,
the homicide numbers are pretty high.

00:17:42.610 --> 00:17:46.041
Roughly 60 per 100,000.

00:17:46.108 --> 00:17:50.481
Which puts Flint
above Memphis, that's per capita,

00:17:51.122 --> 00:17:53.303
Chicago, Detroit,

00:17:54.350 --> 00:17:55.597
but not St. Louis.

00:17:55.664 --> 00:17:59.883
St. Louis seems to get the top place.
I guess, in recent years.

00:18:00.470 --> 00:18:03.942
The homicides take place,
I'm sure, all over the city,

00:18:04.009 --> 00:18:08.198
but they're gonna happen
in zip codes like this,

00:18:08.265 --> 00:18:11.957
where it is the hood,
as everyone in town told me.

00:18:12.024 --> 00:18:14.774
Said careful up there on the north side.

00:18:15.821 --> 00:18:19.954
And what I've always found in the hood is
most people are friendly and cool,

00:18:20.021 --> 00:18:22.637
and they're actually happy
you're interested in them.

00:18:22.704 --> 00:18:24.837
They want to tell their stories.

00:18:25.273 --> 00:18:27.757
And then there are
some downright sketchy people

00:18:27.824 --> 00:18:29.484
that you don't want to be around.

00:18:29.551 --> 00:18:31.701
So you always gotta
keep flowing and moving,

00:18:31.768 --> 00:18:34.968
especially when you got a camera
and look like me in these parts.

00:18:35.035 --> 00:18:37.958
So, yeah, we'll scope around.

00:18:38.025 --> 00:18:41.739
I want to show you some of this
real estate because Flint's real estate

00:18:41.806 --> 00:18:44.596
is rock bottom as far as prices go.

00:18:44.663 --> 00:18:47.331
And I found a couple houses
on Zillow last night

00:18:47.398 --> 00:18:50.903
where if someone wants
to get a home for the price of rent,

00:18:50.970 --> 00:18:54.176
say for half a year most places,
you can have a home in Flint.

00:18:54.243 --> 00:18:57.880
Now, do you just want to
put up with the rough part of Flint.

00:18:58.422 --> 00:19:01.595
So, we're at the fire station,
honor, serve, and unite.

00:19:01.662 --> 00:19:05.513
Again, the murals
just keep on giving in this town.

00:19:06.423 --> 00:19:08.184
It is quite cool.

00:19:08.251 --> 00:19:11.401
And so we're getting out of
this place where you gotta remember,

00:19:11.468 --> 00:19:13.581
200,000 people were here at one time.

00:19:13.655 --> 00:19:16.750
And so a lot of these lots
were probably homes at one time,

00:19:16.817 --> 00:19:19.484
and they've just leveled them over time.

00:19:19.992 --> 00:19:24.301
So you get these big open places
in cities like Flint or Detroit.

00:19:24.368 --> 00:19:28.263
In Detroit they're doing a lot of gardens
which I find to be quite cool.

00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:33.089
Look at this, though. You have
that remnants of a thriving economy.

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:37.452
And then you can just see how things
just sort of fell out here

00:19:38.459 --> 00:19:39.292
and rubble.

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:43.112
Look at that chimney.
It is formerly a house, obviously.

00:19:43.780 --> 00:19:47.947
And then some people raising
their families and doing all right.

00:19:48.014 --> 00:19:51.553
So definitely a different America out here

00:19:52.087 --> 00:19:53.859
compared to most of it.

00:19:53.926 --> 00:19:56.463
And this is what makes
interesting content.

00:19:56.530 --> 00:19:59.582
Unfortunately, middle-class suburbs
aren't that interesting.

00:19:59.649 --> 00:20:03.190
Neighborhood after neighborhood
and that is what a lot of America is

00:20:03.257 --> 00:20:06.868
but places like this
where you have these architectural gems

00:20:06.935 --> 00:20:10.015
and then you see the plywood,
and then you see the blight.

00:20:10.082 --> 00:20:12.822
And it's a bit sketchy.
So it makes it interesting.

00:20:12.889 --> 00:20:15.969
And so I'll get comments
in this video saying

00:20:16.036 --> 00:20:19.594
"America is over it's all done.
It's all falling apart and over."

00:20:19.661 --> 00:20:20.932
Well, no, it's not.

00:20:20.999 --> 00:20:23.576
There are plenty of
thriving places in this country.

00:20:23.643 --> 00:20:26.346
Everything is going through a cycle.
Flint, especially,

00:20:26.413 --> 00:20:28.129
is an extreme form of that, right?

00:20:28.196 --> 00:20:30.176
Where it had its high point.

00:20:31.337 --> 00:20:34.075
So let's try to find some people here.

00:20:34.142 --> 00:20:37.543
This is actually cooler
than I thought it would be

00:20:37.610 --> 00:20:40.077
as far as buildings and architecture.

00:20:41.270 --> 00:20:45.184
You just have roads like this
where they have barricaded it off.

00:20:45.251 --> 00:20:47.213
So no one can drive down them.

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:49.013
We've got a big park here.

00:20:49.864 --> 00:20:51.918
You know, from what I'm seeing in Flint,

00:20:51.985 --> 00:20:54.376
there is money here coming into the city

00:20:54.443 --> 00:20:57.653
as far as the YMCA, big parks.

00:20:57.720 --> 00:21:00.808
I can't speak credibly on it
because I don't live here

00:21:00.875 --> 00:21:04.022
but even in America's roughest places,

00:21:04.089 --> 00:21:06.300
there are always resources coming through.

00:21:06.367 --> 00:21:08.517
Now, I'm not saying
it's fixing the problem.

00:21:08.584 --> 00:21:10.800
And quite often money is the easy part.

00:21:10.867 --> 00:21:15.467
That's what's wild. You think money
and buildings will change a place.

00:21:16.230 --> 00:21:20.630
It's like, how do you bring up
people's sense of purpose and pride?

00:21:21.823 --> 00:21:24.276
The pride that comes from
work and earning money

00:21:24.343 --> 00:21:26.143
and supporting your family.

00:21:27.093 --> 00:21:29.470
That's a tougher challenge, right?

00:21:29.537 --> 00:21:31.987
You can throw
all the money you want at something.

00:21:32.054 --> 00:21:35.233
But if the mentality is not there
then it's hard to fix.

00:21:35.300 --> 00:21:39.032
And I'm not saying the mentality
is not here. I don't know anyone here.

00:21:39.099 --> 00:21:43.332
But I'm just saying in other parts of
the country that are pretty down and out

00:21:43.399 --> 00:21:47.841
there's a lot of cycles
of poverty and rough upbringings.

00:21:47.908 --> 00:21:50.195
and that is the heavy lift.

00:21:50.810 --> 00:21:54.374
The easy lift is actually buildings.
It takes all of it though really.

00:21:55.624 --> 00:21:58.383
Okay, we're coming up to this house soon.

00:22:01.420 --> 00:22:02.815
Okay, here it is.

00:22:03.942 --> 00:22:06.855
A bit of a fixer-upper
but you won't believe the price.

00:22:07.430 --> 00:22:11.497
You're getting a fair amount
of land there, a nice corner lot.

00:22:12.348 --> 00:22:15.207
I don't know
if that's going to be fixed up.

00:22:15.274 --> 00:22:16.227
Had its day.

00:22:16.756 --> 00:22:18.991
But here we go. Two beds, two baths.

00:22:19.499 --> 00:22:21.550
992 square feet.

00:22:22.555 --> 00:22:27.246
With the price of $6,500.

00:22:27.313 --> 00:22:28.513
That can be yours.

00:22:29.360 --> 00:22:33.730
And I'm sure you could buy
this one for maybe even less.

00:22:35.326 --> 00:22:36.459
And there you go.

00:22:37.113 --> 00:22:40.827
Your Flintstone dream
comes true on the cheap.

00:22:41.720 --> 00:22:44.965
Now these were all GM workers

00:22:45.032 --> 00:22:47.114
or a business aligned with GM.

00:22:47.880 --> 00:22:48.733
There you go.

00:22:48.800 --> 00:22:52.763
A month of rent basically,
in a decent place in New York City

00:22:52.830 --> 00:22:55.138
will get you into Flint.

00:22:55.709 --> 00:22:59.286
Now, do you want to live in Flint
is another question

00:22:59.353 --> 00:23:02.855
and what are you going to do here?
But hey, there you go.

00:23:03.390 --> 00:23:05.394
You want cheap real estate?

00:23:05.461 --> 00:23:06.294
There it is.

00:23:08.462 --> 00:23:10.720
Here we have a much nicer one.

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:14.217
Four bed, two bath, 2,400 square feet.

00:23:14.713 --> 00:23:19.947
This one is going for
many multiples more at $80,000.

00:23:20.613 --> 00:23:21.480
There you go.

00:23:22.320 --> 00:23:26.249
What's that mortgage payment
with the taxes here and insurance?

00:23:26.316 --> 00:23:29.770
I'm going to guess 800 bucks,
something like that.

00:23:31.940 --> 00:23:35.553
[Peter] So you work for USPS
and you moved out here, cheap rent.

00:23:35.620 --> 00:23:37.234
[woman] Yeah, I pay $850 a month.

00:23:37.301 --> 00:23:38.273
[Peter] $850?

00:23:38.340 --> 00:23:42.333
[woman] Yeah, but with my credit,
the house was about $80,000.

00:23:42.420 --> 00:23:45.842
I think it would have been $1,000
I would have had to come up with.

00:23:45.909 --> 00:23:47.306
-Okay.
-And I had it.

00:23:47.373 --> 00:23:49.471
I mean I still do
but you know what I mean?

00:23:49.538 --> 00:23:54.853
It's just to qualify for the program 
you have to have six-something or better.

00:23:54.920 --> 00:23:56.220
My credit took a flaw.

00:23:56.287 --> 00:23:58.954
-I have a truck in that garage, so…
-Okay.

00:23:59.593 --> 00:24:00.426
You know.

00:24:00.810 --> 00:24:02.229
-But thanks for what you do.

00:24:02.296 --> 00:24:03.722
-Oh, thank you.
-I like USPS.

00:24:03.789 --> 00:24:05.933
You guys never mess up for me at least.

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:08.219
Well, mail's gonna be behind a little bit.

00:24:08.286 --> 00:24:11.665
They fired the company that was
picking up the mail just so you know.

00:24:11.732 --> 00:24:12.894
-In Flint?
-Yeah.

00:24:13.319 --> 00:24:15.963
-Oh.
-So there's going to be delayed mail now.

00:24:16.030 --> 00:24:19.099
-So you're running out here
'cause it's cheap? It works for you?

00:24:19.166 --> 00:24:20.533
Yeah. I'm a single mom, so…

00:24:20.600 --> 00:24:22.004
-Okay.
-You know what I mean?

00:24:22.071 --> 00:24:24.383
How's the energy in the hood here?
People cool?

00:24:24.450 --> 00:24:26.492
Is it much danger and much violence or?

00:24:26.559 --> 00:24:28.846
No. This street is pretty quiet.

00:24:28.913 --> 00:24:30.293
-This street's quiet?
-Yeah.

00:24:30.360 --> 00:24:32.933
If you go down,
it depends on what block you're on.

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:36.431
Some people usually keep to their self
and they keep to their self.

00:24:36.498 --> 00:24:38.632
I don't have any in commons with them.

00:24:38.699 --> 00:24:39.935
They're quiet.

00:24:40.002 --> 00:24:41.263
And that's what I wanted.

00:24:41.330 --> 00:24:44.653
I just wanted I keep to myself
and I just wanted peace, so…

00:24:44.720 --> 00:24:45.973
-Okay. Okay.

00:24:46.040 --> 00:24:49.690
And I think there's a lot of older people
that stay around here, you know.

00:24:49.757 --> 00:24:52.516
Old people from back in the day of GM?

00:24:52.583 --> 00:24:54.379
-Maybe.
-Maybe. Okay.

00:24:54.446 --> 00:24:55.279
Wow.

00:24:55.346 --> 00:24:57.883
There's the old man
over there at the corner.

00:24:57.950 --> 00:25:00.983
He was just outside
cutting his yard, you know.

00:25:01.050 --> 00:25:01.883
That's cool.

00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:06.576
Let me ask you this, as a single mother
making what you make at USPS,

00:25:06.643 --> 00:25:09.776
living out here,
can you pay all the bills okay?

00:25:10.301 --> 00:25:11.788
Or is it tight?

00:25:11.855 --> 00:25:13.013
I get by.

00:25:13.080 --> 00:25:15.495
You have to be smart.

00:25:15.562 --> 00:25:17.063
You have to budget.

00:25:17.130 --> 00:25:19.730
I have teenagers,
so they're wanting a lot of things.

00:25:19.797 --> 00:25:21.264
We're just getting by.

00:25:22.024 --> 00:25:25.497
The water is actually with that
type of size house, it's not that bad.

00:25:25.564 --> 00:25:27.210
-The what? I'm sorry?
-The water.

00:25:27.277 --> 00:25:30.241
-The water bill isn't high.
-Your water bill's not high?

00:25:30.308 --> 00:25:31.193
It's under $100.

00:25:31.260 --> 00:25:34.960
-I mean, that's a pretty amazing deal.
If you can get your credit straight.

00:25:35.027 --> 00:25:36.729
Would you love to live here?

00:25:37.284 --> 00:25:38.485
It might be or whatever…

00:25:38.552 --> 00:25:42.073
Regardless, I know what I'm looking for.
Like foundation, roofing.

00:25:42.140 --> 00:25:44.749
When it comes to buying,
I know what I'm looking for.

00:25:44.816 --> 00:25:47.560
And I'm not going to settle
until they fix it.

00:25:47.627 --> 00:25:49.698
I won't buy until things are fixed.

00:25:49.764 --> 00:25:52.657
-Smart. 'Cause that stuff gets expensive.
-Yeah. I know.

00:25:52.724 --> 00:25:54.764
Trust me. I already know. [chuckles]

00:25:55.437 --> 00:25:57.969
Thank you. All the best.
Good luck with everything.

00:25:58.036 --> 00:25:59.503
-Thank you.
-Thank you.

00:26:07.040 --> 00:26:10.433
[Peter] Give credit where credit's due.
They're really trying here.

00:26:10.500 --> 00:26:12.767
Look at all these flower planters.

00:26:13.669 --> 00:26:15.269
You can see around here.

00:26:16.247 --> 00:26:18.016
Genesee County Preschool.

00:26:18.083 --> 00:26:21.043
Actually quite a bit
of park area in Flint.

00:26:21.110 --> 00:26:22.172
And this is the hood.

00:26:22.239 --> 00:26:25.659
This is what I was told
was the rough part.

00:26:26.450 --> 00:26:28.889
But even in this part of town,

00:26:29.356 --> 00:26:32.328
look, all the murals up on the buildings.

00:26:33.100 --> 00:26:35.783
And we got a blast from the past here.

00:26:35.850 --> 00:26:36.717
An old phone.

00:26:39.060 --> 00:26:43.120
For those that know, you remember
exactly how these numbers press

00:26:43.187 --> 00:26:46.932
and how they're indented
and your finger goes into them.

00:26:46.999 --> 00:26:49.266
And that feeling and that feeling.

00:26:53.004 --> 00:26:55.335
And those who don't
will never experience that.

00:26:55.402 --> 00:26:58.202
But that's how it was done
back in the day.

00:27:01.259 --> 00:27:04.781
Looks like we've got
an old elementary or high school.

00:27:04.848 --> 00:27:05.681
No longer.

00:27:06.951 --> 00:27:10.400
You gotta think
what that does to the school system

00:27:10.467 --> 00:27:13.302
when that many kids leave town.

00:27:15.900 --> 00:27:18.013
Well, they just let it go

00:27:19.007 --> 00:27:20.574
to this I guess.

00:27:20.641 --> 00:27:22.774
Looks like a dumping ground now.

00:27:24.781 --> 00:27:25.614
Geez.

00:27:26.782 --> 00:27:28.782
All sorts of stuff back there.

00:27:29.886 --> 00:27:30.908
That's awful.

00:27:30.975 --> 00:27:33.506
You can see there's some fires lit.

00:27:33.573 --> 00:27:38.262
And that's all across from
actually a nice looking playground.

00:27:38.329 --> 00:27:40.458
[siren blares in the distance]

00:27:40.525 --> 00:27:42.340
And you hear a lot of that in Flint.

00:27:42.407 --> 00:27:43.451
The sirens.

00:27:45.019 --> 00:27:47.866
You know, not a  good visual every day.

00:27:49.940 --> 00:27:53.114
And how do you
teach people not to do that?

00:27:53.754 --> 00:27:55.923
Because here's the deal.
If your dad does it

00:27:55.990 --> 00:27:57.567
then you do it most likely.

00:27:57.634 --> 00:27:59.101
No matter what that is.

00:27:59.652 --> 00:28:04.483
Throwing trash on the ground
or how you treat servers at a restaurant.

00:28:04.550 --> 00:28:07.391
It usually comes from the parenting.

00:28:08.161 --> 00:28:12.408
So how do you change the cycle
so that's no longer?

00:28:14.626 --> 00:28:18.053
You know, I can only go so far
as an outsider here.

00:28:18.120 --> 00:28:19.403
I'm looking for people.

00:28:19.470 --> 00:28:20.353
There are really no…

00:28:20.420 --> 00:28:23.188
There's nobody
on the streets unfortunately.

00:28:23.724 --> 00:28:26.385
Here we are, Bryant Elementary School.

00:28:26.452 --> 00:28:27.785
School of Fine Arts.

00:28:29.046 --> 00:28:34.536
Bryant Center has been properly looted.

00:28:34.603 --> 00:28:37.183
And then the DG's come into town.

00:28:37.250 --> 00:28:38.824
DG is everywhere.

00:28:41.785 --> 00:28:43.925
[Peter] Look at you just styling.

00:28:44.461 --> 00:28:46.058
Look at that. There we go.

00:28:46.125 --> 00:28:47.454
So you thought through it.

00:28:47.521 --> 00:28:51.191
You went glasses, to lipstick,
all the way through the dress.

00:28:51.699 --> 00:28:52.532
There we go.

00:28:53.364 --> 00:28:56.655
And those nails, those nails
might be weapons. Can I see those?

00:28:56.722 --> 00:28:58.758
No, those are not weapons.

00:28:59.439 --> 00:29:00.953
-What's your name?
-Cassandra.

00:29:01.020 --> 00:29:02.946
Cassandra. Nice to meet you, Peter.

00:29:03.013 --> 00:29:04.581
-Peter.
-Yeah. Nice to meet you.

00:29:04.648 --> 00:29:05.679
What do you do?

00:29:05.746 --> 00:29:07.517
I make videos all over the country.

00:29:07.584 --> 00:29:09.977
-You do?
-And I look for cool locals to talk to.

00:29:10.044 --> 00:29:12.060
Oh my goodness. That's amazing.

00:29:12.127 --> 00:29:14.747
So what are the topic today
on this beautiful Sunday?

00:29:14.814 --> 00:29:18.114
You know, there's no narrative to fill.
Just learn from the people.

00:29:18.182 --> 00:29:19.992
-Go with the flow.
-Go with the flow.

00:29:20.059 --> 00:29:21.034
Are you from Flint?

00:29:21.101 --> 00:29:23.838
So I'm originally from Louisiana,
Baton Rouge.

00:29:23.905 --> 00:29:26.310
-Okay. You are? Nice.
-Yeah.

00:29:26.377 --> 00:29:28.409
-What do you think of it up here?

00:29:28.476 --> 00:29:32.619
Oh, I just think we need more
love and great hospitality here.

00:29:32.686 --> 00:29:35.233
I think there's no unity here no more.

00:29:35.300 --> 00:29:36.436
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:29:36.503 --> 00:29:39.962
Like, when you go down South,
it's nothing but family,

00:29:40.029 --> 00:29:41.693
and it's a lot of love.

00:29:41.760 --> 00:29:43.522
People just come together.

00:29:43.589 --> 00:29:45.754
No matter what race you are,

00:29:45.821 --> 00:29:49.584
no matter if you're old, young,
everybody just gets together.

00:29:49.651 --> 00:29:51.827
-You're not feeling that in Flint?
-I'm not.

00:29:51.894 --> 00:29:53.120
-Really?
-I'm not.

00:29:53.186 --> 00:29:55.950
But, like, when we have events and stuff,

00:29:56.017 --> 00:29:58.267
that's the only time
that we can get together.

00:29:58.340 --> 00:30:01.245
And then when
we do have the events it's like

00:30:01.312 --> 00:30:05.809
you got to be cautious
of how many young people is around you.

00:30:05.876 --> 00:30:08.353
Because you're older
and you really don't want to,

00:30:08.420 --> 00:30:10.121
you know, get shot up.

00:30:10.188 --> 00:30:12.905
Or you don't want to be in
the midst of anybody's beef.

00:30:12.972 --> 00:30:15.543
Because it's so young-minded these days.

00:30:15.610 --> 00:30:18.883
The generation and the era now is just…
[scoffs]

00:30:18.950 --> 00:30:19.783
-Right.

00:30:20.430 --> 00:30:22.193
-I'm sorry.
-It's crude.

00:30:22.260 --> 00:30:26.286
-Well, look. I run into
great young guys and girls all the time.

00:30:26.353 --> 00:30:28.343
But, it's interesting.

00:30:28.410 --> 00:30:29.943
In inner-city America,

00:30:31.058 --> 00:30:32.942
there's no order anymore.

00:30:33.009 --> 00:30:36.520
Before, like, say, Bloods and Crips,
there was order from the top down.

00:30:36.587 --> 00:30:38.452
-Yeah.
-You didn't disrespect your OG.

00:30:38.519 --> 00:30:40.698
-And we have no order. 
-You stayed in line.

00:30:40.765 --> 00:30:43.466
Now everyone's scared of
the 15-year-old with the gun.

00:30:43.533 --> 00:30:45.757
-Yeah. That's what I'm saying!
-TikTok brain.

00:30:45.824 --> 00:30:48.946
-That's what I'm saying.
-And they don't know the consequences.

00:30:49.013 --> 00:30:52.495
They haven't fully developed
their brains, gotten good examples.

00:30:52.562 --> 00:30:55.527
And then it's like
that's what you got to watch out for.

00:30:55.594 --> 00:30:58.579
-And they have been traumatized.
-100%.

00:30:58.646 --> 00:31:02.707
The upbringing has been
raised off of survival.

00:31:02.774 --> 00:31:04.499
-Yeah.
-And no love, like…

00:31:04.566 --> 00:31:06.513
-Yeah. Right.

00:31:06.580 --> 00:31:08.614
-So, that has to change.
-It does.

00:31:08.681 --> 00:31:11.219
-And that's not a money thing, either.
-It's not.

00:31:11.286 --> 00:31:14.889
But it's more of, like, time and love.
Like you're saying. And nurturing.

00:31:14.956 --> 00:31:16.277
-Yes, it is.
-Connection.

00:31:16.344 --> 00:31:19.873
-But you seem like you got a lot of love.
-I do. I have a lot of love.

00:31:19.940 --> 00:31:22.563
-You got kids?
-Yes, I do. I have two beautiful boys.

00:31:22.630 --> 00:31:24.190
-Okay. Here?
-Seven and 12. Yes.

00:31:24.257 --> 00:31:27.172
-Oh, good for you.
-And they're the light of my life.

00:31:27.239 --> 00:31:31.260
Like they really made me grow
in so many different ways.

00:31:31.327 --> 00:31:34.895
Like just when I first held my oldest,

00:31:35.395 --> 00:31:37.230
it was like a new start for me.

00:31:37.297 --> 00:31:39.490
A fresh start at life.

00:31:39.557 --> 00:31:42.631
Like I knew some of the things
that I was doing like partying,

00:31:42.698 --> 00:31:44.062
I had to change.

00:31:44.129 --> 00:31:45.862
I had to change my mental.

00:31:46.798 --> 00:31:48.737
And I just grew out of everything.

00:31:48.804 --> 00:31:51.118
And now I am so refreshed

00:31:51.570 --> 00:31:54.113
and rejuvenated now that I have children.

00:31:54.180 --> 00:31:57.080
-That was priority
and everything else fell to the wayside.

00:31:57.147 --> 00:32:00.259
-That's a choice of a parent.
-Yes, it is.

00:32:00.326 --> 00:32:02.498
And it's a beautiful choice to make.
It is.

00:32:02.565 --> 00:32:05.260
-How do you keep your kids
from getting into

00:32:05.327 --> 00:32:07.018
the heavy, dark stuff out here?

00:32:07.085 --> 00:32:11.725
Okay, so keeping them
involved in educational activities.

00:32:12.365 --> 00:32:17.421
And my sons are in Christianity activities
throughout the church.

00:32:17.488 --> 00:32:19.680
We do drives. We do picnics.

00:32:19.747 --> 00:32:23.206
Anything to keep them
out of the streets and focus.

00:32:23.766 --> 00:32:26.371
Basketball camp, summer camp.

00:32:27.119 --> 00:32:31.193
I have thrived all year
to make sure

00:32:31.260 --> 00:32:34.842
that I keep my kids
out of the streets and away from violence.

00:32:34.909 --> 00:32:38.567
Anything that I can do positive,
lemonade stands to cookie stands,

00:32:38.634 --> 00:32:39.834
we have done.

00:32:40.873 --> 00:32:42.633
And we're going to keep doing it.

00:32:42.700 --> 00:32:45.451
-Are you a single mother?
-So yes, I am a single mother.

00:32:45.518 --> 00:32:47.801
-That's tough, huh?
-Well, it is tough.

00:32:47.868 --> 00:32:50.662
But at the end of the day,
if you have Christ

00:32:50.729 --> 00:32:54.998
or whoever positive that you are
praying to, you will get through it.

00:32:55.549 --> 00:32:56.821
You will get through it.

00:32:56.888 --> 00:33:00.646
-It makes you stronger too.
-Yes it does make me a lot stronger.

00:33:00.713 --> 00:33:03.628
Because at the end of the day,
I want them to know

00:33:03.695 --> 00:33:07.455
that when they get older,
their women, wives,

00:33:07.522 --> 00:33:11.602
are supposed to be great people of society

00:33:11.669 --> 00:33:13.906
and also lead them in a positive way.

00:33:13.973 --> 00:33:17.932
You know, just like men are supposed to
lead their wives in a positive way.

00:33:17.999 --> 00:33:21.633
So I think it's great to be
just great people all the way around.

00:33:21.700 --> 00:33:26.600
I feel like whatever you show them,
that's what they're going to possess.

00:33:26.667 --> 00:33:29.284
That's the type of characteristics
they're going to have.

00:33:29.351 --> 00:33:32.141
Try to create a good example.

00:33:32.666 --> 00:33:37.156
-So what's the solution to hoods
like this in Flint, across America?

00:33:37.223 --> 00:33:39.689
-Well, I would say…
-What are your thoughts?

00:33:39.756 --> 00:33:42.355
I would say just getting together

00:33:42.873 --> 00:33:45.940
and wanting to do
a whole sweep of cleaning up,

00:33:46.494 --> 00:33:48.631
doing projects together,

00:33:48.698 --> 00:33:51.521
and just making our side great again.

00:33:52.429 --> 00:33:56.246
'Cause that's pride, right? When you
keep your place clean and it looks good.

00:33:56.313 --> 00:33:57.146
Yeah.

00:33:57.213 --> 00:34:00.414
And so if a kid grows up
and there's trash everywhere like here,

00:34:01.354 --> 00:34:02.421
they don't know.

00:34:02.873 --> 00:34:04.820
-Right?
-Yeah, right. That's it.

00:34:04.887 --> 00:34:08.157
They're not going to feel pride
and there's broken windows theory.

00:34:08.224 --> 00:34:10.713
If you see broken glass and trash,
you're going to feel more trash.

00:34:10.780 --> 00:34:13.574
That's it. It's going to keep multiplying.

00:34:13.641 --> 00:34:14.474
That's it.

00:34:15.303 --> 00:34:18.224
So if you give them
the clean mind state of,

00:34:18.291 --> 00:34:22.529
"Hey, let's go out here
and do a neighborhood clean up."

00:34:22.596 --> 00:34:25.046
Then the kids will be more like,

00:34:25.113 --> 00:34:27.913
"Wow, we gotta keep
our environment clean."

00:34:28.413 --> 00:34:31.093
But if you just throw trash
in front of them,

00:34:31.160 --> 00:34:34.743
I mean, what do you think
they're naturally going to do?

00:34:38.621 --> 00:34:42.662
I could imagine if you're a kid here,
like Miss Queen said,

00:34:43.609 --> 00:34:47.523
you better hope you have someone
like Miss Queen getting you into sports,

00:34:47.590 --> 00:34:49.457
into activities, into camps.

00:34:50.104 --> 00:34:52.634
If you're into religion.

00:34:53.801 --> 00:34:54.634
Because

00:34:55.980 --> 00:34:58.713
the other side of things here is just like

00:34:59.446 --> 00:35:00.792
bad influences

00:35:02.066 --> 00:35:03.831
turning you on to drugs, crime,

00:35:04.546 --> 00:35:05.679
murdering people.

00:35:06.595 --> 00:35:11.076
And then once you go down that road,
it's very hard to come back from it.

00:35:11.143 --> 00:35:12.610
Though I've seen that happen.

00:35:12.677 --> 00:35:15.954
I've seen…
I've met people that did their time.

00:35:16.734 --> 00:35:22.184
Prison, came out the other side
and became productive members of society.

00:35:22.251 --> 00:35:23.384
That does happen.

00:35:24.193 --> 00:35:25.893
But it's a hard slog.

00:35:27.033 --> 00:35:30.171
Better not to go down
that road in the first place.

00:35:30.238 --> 00:35:32.618
Let's just cruise
another street here or two

00:35:32.685 --> 00:35:36.781
and then we'll go over to
the nice part of town.

00:35:36.848 --> 00:35:40.181
Which I heard there is
a nice part of town in Flint

00:35:40.787 --> 00:35:45.410
where the upper level managers
for GM lived back in the day.

00:35:45.477 --> 00:35:47.135
I don't know who lives there now,

00:35:47.202 --> 00:35:50.883
but I'm sure it was like the attorneys,
the accountants, the doctors,

00:35:51.735 --> 00:35:53.602
people with bigger salaries.

00:35:54.493 --> 00:35:57.081
And even in a small city like Flint,

00:35:57.148 --> 00:35:59.314
you can live in your own bubble here.

00:35:59.381 --> 00:36:03.581
Where where we're going
I'm sure they never come over here ever.

00:36:04.455 --> 00:36:07.652
And the same, they're not
coming from here over to there.

00:36:07.719 --> 00:36:10.847
Almost like parallel universes.

00:36:12.473 --> 00:36:13.955
But they're doing a good job.

00:36:14.022 --> 00:36:16.194
I will give Flint credit
for all the mowing

00:36:16.261 --> 00:36:20.092
and all of these open spaces
which I'm sure were houses like that.

00:36:20.159 --> 00:36:22.609
They're keeping maintained.
I'll give them credit.

00:36:22.676 --> 00:36:27.234
So good job Flint on that front
because this would just all grow up.

00:36:27.301 --> 00:36:29.753
Actually just turn into forest, I think.

00:36:41.123 --> 00:36:44.069
-[woman] This is some of the ribs.
-[Peter] Oh yeah.

00:36:44.136 --> 00:36:45.729
-This is pro.
-Brisket.

00:36:45.796 --> 00:36:48.146
-I'll do brisket. Thank you.
-Yeah.

00:36:48.213 --> 00:36:49.663
Try the tips next time.

00:36:49.730 --> 00:36:52.700
Now we're only open until
September 4th, Labor Day.

00:36:52.767 --> 00:36:55.148
That's our last day of the season.

00:36:55.215 --> 00:36:56.441
-Oh.
-We're seasonal.

00:36:56.508 --> 00:36:58.683
-Okay. What do you do after that?

00:36:58.750 --> 00:36:59.605
Teaching.

00:36:59.672 --> 00:37:01.169
-You're a teacher?
-Yes.

00:37:01.236 --> 00:37:02.069
-Thank you.

00:37:02.540 --> 00:37:03.473
Yes, thank you.

00:37:03.540 --> 00:37:06.220
-I'm all over the country
and the story I hear the most

00:37:06.287 --> 00:37:08.533
is teachers leaving unfortunately.

00:37:08.600 --> 00:37:09.782
I know they are.

00:37:09.849 --> 00:37:13.648
I'm a preschool teacher, so I work closely
with the kindergarten teachers.

00:37:13.715 --> 00:37:14.548
-Okay.

00:37:14.615 --> 00:37:16.751
And they are tired.

00:37:17.520 --> 00:37:19.372
Kindergarten teachers are very tired.

00:37:19.439 --> 00:37:20.913
-The kindergarten teachers are tired?

00:37:20.980 --> 00:37:25.571
We try our best as preschool teachers
to prepare them for kindergarten.

00:37:25.638 --> 00:37:28.445
Okay. So how is it going
for you teaching right now?

00:37:28.512 --> 00:37:30.664
-Oh, it's going wonderful.
-That's cool.

00:37:30.731 --> 00:37:32.795
It's been 28 years. I thank God.

00:37:32.862 --> 00:37:35.429
And without him, I couldn't do it.

00:37:36.212 --> 00:37:38.557
-God guides you through everything?
-He does.

00:37:38.624 --> 00:37:42.966
Even when they are struggling
or they have behaviors,

00:37:43.433 --> 00:37:45.593
I know where my help comes from.

00:37:45.660 --> 00:37:48.013
-You're trying to
teach them social and what?

00:37:48.080 --> 00:37:49.285
Emotional.

00:37:49.352 --> 00:37:51.714
Because if they can't do that,
they can't learn.

00:37:51.781 --> 00:37:54.515
If they cannot learn
how to control their behaviors,

00:37:54.589 --> 00:37:57.162
or how to problem solve,

00:37:57.623 --> 00:38:02.479
or how to calm themselves down,

00:38:02.546 --> 00:38:05.264
social, emotional,
you know, instead of throwing stuff,

00:38:05.331 --> 00:38:07.492
instead of using profanity,

00:38:07.559 --> 00:38:11.398
instead of, you know,
if they can't do that, they can't learn.

00:38:11.465 --> 00:38:15.665
All that ABCs and one two threes,
and all that comes after that.

00:38:16.286 --> 00:38:19.493
They have to know
how to interact with each other.

00:38:21.695 --> 00:38:23.803
[Peter] So Pat's got it all laid out here.

00:38:23.870 --> 00:38:27.913
I can't put the camera on it because
it's her trade secret that nobody knows.

00:38:27.980 --> 00:38:30.977
-Which keeps people coming back, right?
-Yes.

00:38:31.044 --> 00:38:33.311
Oh, yeah. I see what you're doing.

00:38:34.179 --> 00:38:35.345
Yeah.

00:38:35.412 --> 00:38:37.583
-That's a good trick.
-Thank you.

00:38:37.650 --> 00:38:40.144
-What's your take on Flint?
Do you love Flint or…

00:38:40.211 --> 00:38:41.693
I do. I do.

00:38:41.760 --> 00:38:43.293
I love the people here.

00:38:43.859 --> 00:38:45.681
I love my community.

00:38:46.746 --> 00:38:48.528
I'm proud of my community.

00:38:48.595 --> 00:38:53.127
We do have a lot of neighbors
that they take care of their property.

00:38:53.194 --> 00:38:57.163
You're still going to have some
that don't, but there's a lot that do.

00:38:57.230 --> 00:38:59.833
I moved here in 1990 from Tennessee.

00:38:59.900 --> 00:39:00.892
-Okay.

00:39:00.959 --> 00:39:03.419
I still have a lot of
my Southern roots, you know.

00:39:03.486 --> 00:39:05.043
I like to speak to people.

00:39:05.110 --> 00:39:06.213
-Yeah, I can see that.

00:39:06.280 --> 00:39:08.334
A lot of northerners don't like to speak,

00:39:08.401 --> 00:39:11.531
but if they give me eye contact,
I'm going to say hello.

00:39:11.598 --> 00:39:14.646
-Ms. Pat, you seem like
an optimistic, happy person.

00:39:14.713 --> 00:39:18.353
What do you have to say
to your fellow Americans right now?

00:39:18.420 --> 00:39:21.689
We need to love on each other
and respect one another.

00:39:22.254 --> 00:39:23.103
We do.

00:39:23.170 --> 00:39:26.189
And we need to pray
for our leaders that God will

00:39:26.256 --> 00:39:29.549
give them the wisdom
and knowledge to lead us correctly

00:39:29.616 --> 00:39:33.080
according to God's will
and not their will.

00:39:33.147 --> 00:39:34.480
That's what we need.

00:39:35.870 --> 00:39:38.058
-There we go. That's the final product.

00:39:38.125 --> 00:39:40.195
We couldn't show the process.

00:39:40.262 --> 00:39:42.462
-Lots of love in there.
-Lots of love.

00:39:42.529 --> 00:39:44.592
All right. I can't wait to try this.

00:39:44.659 --> 00:39:45.492
Thank you.

00:39:47.658 --> 00:39:52.753
There we go. Look at that.
Ms. Pat's special brisket.

00:39:53.587 --> 00:39:58.054
It's got a lot of sauce going on here,
but that meat is very tender.

00:40:00.264 --> 00:40:01.731
It's going to be messy

00:40:02.782 --> 00:40:04.649
and you just got to jump in.

00:40:04.715 --> 00:40:06.839
Mmm. That is fantastic.

00:40:08.926 --> 00:40:11.059
There we go. There's a lot here.

00:40:12.972 --> 00:40:13.805
Mmm.

00:40:17.904 --> 00:40:19.113
Very tender meat.

00:40:19.180 --> 00:40:20.652
World-class barbecue.

00:40:21.150 --> 00:40:23.032
-Ms. Pat, you're the best.
-Thank you.

00:40:23.099 --> 00:40:24.566
-Thank you.
-Thank you.

00:40:26.152 --> 00:40:27.423
Yeah. Appreciate it.

00:40:27.490 --> 00:40:29.823
-Have fun with school.
-Yes. Yes.

00:40:29.890 --> 00:40:32.741
[Motown music]

00:40:55.633 --> 00:40:58.166
Wow. Look at this grand old place.

00:40:58.753 --> 00:41:00.189
Vines are taking it over.

00:41:00.740 --> 00:41:04.988
And now the homes are
getting a lot nicer up here on the hill

00:41:05.055 --> 00:41:08.189
across from that massive Catholic school.

00:41:08.842 --> 00:41:11.173
Okay, here's the one I was looking for.

00:41:11.240 --> 00:41:12.573
This one's for sale.

00:41:13.040 --> 00:41:14.177
Tudor Revival.

00:41:15.056 --> 00:41:19.725
Four beds, six baths, 5,153 square feet.

00:41:20.410 --> 00:41:23.888
for $550,000.

00:41:23.955 --> 00:41:25.057
Look at that beauty.

00:41:25.597 --> 00:41:28.123
And it's got a pool in the back I believe.

00:41:28.190 --> 00:41:30.373
And up here you just have

00:41:31.166 --> 00:41:34.402
well, the other side of the tracks,
that's for sure.

00:41:34.469 --> 00:41:35.911
This is also Flint.

00:41:36.997 --> 00:41:38.733
And these are beauties.

00:41:38.800 --> 00:41:41.737
And I'm sure this part of town is

00:41:42.910 --> 00:41:46.300
pretty safe and quiet back in the day.

00:41:46.367 --> 00:41:50.933
And I'm sure it was the GM higher level
management that lived up here.

00:41:52.217 --> 00:41:55.090
That Catholic school
is absolutely massive.

00:41:55.157 --> 00:41:58.183
So I'm sure a lot of the kids were
going there. Maybe they still are.

00:41:58.250 --> 00:42:01.655
And you just have
a different Flint up here.

00:42:02.503 --> 00:42:05.650
It's just right at
the top of a little hill.

00:42:05.717 --> 00:42:07.517
And then it dead ends here.

00:42:09.343 --> 00:42:13.463
But it's just that one little zone
that is really nice.

00:42:14.900 --> 00:42:18.685
And then we come off the hill
and we're back into it.

00:42:23.759 --> 00:42:26.159
[Peter] All right, guys.
We got super lucky.

00:42:26.919 --> 00:42:29.813
And meeting some fans of the channel.

00:42:29.880 --> 00:42:32.146
-Local Flintstones, right?
-Yes.

00:42:32.213 --> 00:42:35.123
Flintstones that are, what,
sixth generation?

00:42:35.190 --> 00:42:36.533
My children are five now.

00:42:36.600 --> 00:42:39.956
Okay.
And you know the automotive history here.

00:42:40.023 --> 00:42:42.673
-And we're gonna cruise for a little bit.
-Absolutely.

00:42:42.740 --> 00:42:45.658
All right. We scored
'cause my historical knowledge,

00:42:45.725 --> 00:42:47.856
you know, it's reading up online.

00:42:47.923 --> 00:42:50.773
But not really understanding
'cause I didn't grow up here.

00:42:50.840 --> 00:42:51.907
So we have Dean.

00:42:52.922 --> 00:42:54.735
-And we got Jens, right?
-Yep.

00:42:54.802 --> 00:42:56.143
-Jens. All right.

00:42:57.495 --> 00:43:02.083
[Dean] I saw the ghost
of what they built in the post-war era.

00:43:02.150 --> 00:43:05.800
The structures and the organizations
that they created

00:43:05.867 --> 00:43:11.166
to live the best life that
they possibly could after a world war.

00:43:11.233 --> 00:43:14.474
Right? There was reason to celebrate,
to look to the future.

00:43:14.541 --> 00:43:17.292
There was reason to
gather together as a community.

00:43:17.359 --> 00:43:19.674
And build something amazing.
And they did that.

00:43:19.741 --> 00:43:22.725
[Peter] Right.
GM got in on the war effort, right?

00:43:22.792 --> 00:43:24.471
-Yes, they did. Sure.
-Okay.

00:43:24.538 --> 00:43:26.648
-The Hellcat was built here, right?

00:43:26.715 --> 00:43:29.515
On this street about five miles behind us.

00:43:30.509 --> 00:43:33.888
At the former Cadillac plant
they built tanks.

00:43:34.619 --> 00:43:36.314
They tested tanks.

00:43:36.381 --> 00:43:39.955
So a lot of tanks that were developed
for the war were developed

00:43:40.022 --> 00:43:42.453
literally just a few miles
from where we sit.

00:43:42.520 --> 00:43:45.348
All of this was General Motors.

00:43:45.415 --> 00:43:48.381
-Oh, all of this was GM's?
-[Jens] Everywhere you look.

00:43:48.448 --> 00:43:51.736
-All of this landscape out here was GM.

00:43:51.803 --> 00:43:54.571
[Jens] It was
what you would call Buick City.

00:43:55.132 --> 00:44:00.332
It was a complex of
28-plus huge industrial-sized buildings.

00:44:00.399 --> 00:44:02.603
And we're gonna show you some of it.

00:44:02.670 --> 00:44:07.199
-To give it a perspective and scale,
there were 30,000 people

00:44:07.266 --> 00:44:09.375
employed at Buick City at one point.

00:44:09.442 --> 00:44:12.269
Way up to that water tower,
past that water tower,

00:44:12.336 --> 00:44:14.329
all of this was factory.

00:44:14.396 --> 00:44:19.112
Where literally steel and leather
and wood went in one end.

00:44:19.179 --> 00:44:21.082
And cars came out of the other end.

00:44:21.149 --> 00:44:22.847
-Okay. What are these now?

00:44:22.914 --> 00:44:24.931
What are these little utility trucks?

00:44:24.998 --> 00:44:29.778
Who knows? I know that there's
a company called Racer Trust that owns…

00:44:29.845 --> 00:44:31.020
-These are Chevys.
-…the property.

00:44:31.100 --> 00:44:33.916
GM has nothing to do with
all those in the electric…

00:44:33.983 --> 00:44:36.232
-Yeah, these are the electric Chevys.

00:44:36.299 --> 00:44:39.363
GM has nothing to do with this anymore.

00:44:39.430 --> 00:44:44.726
-So in your younger years this was
all a factory and it was just booming.

00:44:44.793 --> 00:44:46.943
-That's correct.
-And everyone was involved?

00:44:47.010 --> 00:44:48.229
Everyone in town?

00:44:48.296 --> 00:44:53.068
Pretty much. Yeah. And this was
just one of probably 22 facilities.

00:44:53.135 --> 00:44:56.648
Maybe not this large,
but large enough to support

00:44:56.715 --> 00:45:00.035
Buick, Olds, Pontiac, Cadillac, Chevrolet.

00:45:00.102 --> 00:45:01.730
-So what was the average wage?

00:45:01.797 --> 00:45:06.119
Like, so those times the wife
was staying home with the kids, right?

00:45:06.186 --> 00:45:08.220
You had a house, you had a car.

00:45:08.287 --> 00:45:12.137
The equivalent would be in the
neighborhood of $70,000 to $90,000 today.

00:45:12.204 --> 00:45:13.159
Today's money.

00:45:13.226 --> 00:45:16.218
Sometimes two people
worked in these factories.

00:45:16.285 --> 00:45:18.234
-But houses were way cheaper, right?

00:45:18.301 --> 00:45:22.083
-Actually houses are cheap here now.
-I think they're still pretty cheap.

00:45:22.150 --> 00:45:26.086
-Then everything picked up and left
in the 70s, early 80s.

00:45:26.153 --> 00:45:28.616
-Isn't that pre-NAFTA?
-Pre-NAFTA. Correct.

00:45:28.683 --> 00:45:29.993
So where did it go?

00:45:30.060 --> 00:45:33.415
Well, it didn't go to Mexico or Canada.

00:45:33.482 --> 00:45:37.094
And General Motors had already
established ventures in Mexico and Canada.

00:45:37.161 --> 00:45:40.757
Originally, they went to
Arlington, Indiana,

00:45:41.474 --> 00:45:44.936
-Missouri, Arlington, Texas, I should say.
-Okay.

00:45:45.003 --> 00:45:46.133
Buffalo, New York.

00:45:46.200 --> 00:45:48.223
Outward, away from the Rust Belt.

00:45:48.290 --> 00:45:50.498
-What was the catalyst?
Why did they leave?

00:45:50.565 --> 00:45:53.864
It's really hard to say
because they built the infrastructure,

00:45:53.931 --> 00:45:56.784
the power systems,
the water systems, the rail systems,

00:45:56.851 --> 00:45:59.319
to support basically what amounts to,

00:45:59.386 --> 00:46:04.521
between Saginaw and Detroit,
a massive manufacturing megacenter.

00:46:05.160 --> 00:46:09.627
My belief is workman's rights,
the expectation of wages.

00:46:09.694 --> 00:46:12.445
When the riots that happened
throughout the Midwest,

00:46:12.512 --> 00:46:15.318
especially in Detroit and Flint happened,

00:46:15.385 --> 00:46:17.081
I think some of those people,

00:46:17.148 --> 00:46:20.783
some of the white-collar management layer
of not just General Motors,

00:46:20.856 --> 00:46:24.137
but every manufacturer,
they walked onto their factory floors

00:46:24.204 --> 00:46:28.170
and realized if these guys decide that
they've had enough of us, we're finished.

00:46:28.237 --> 00:46:29.137
-[laughs]
-Okay.

00:46:29.204 --> 00:46:31.114
I think they moved out quickly.

00:46:31.214 --> 00:46:32.979
And they didn't just move the jobs.

00:46:33.046 --> 00:46:35.533
They moved themselves
and they moved the jobs.

00:46:35.600 --> 00:46:40.013
-Okay. So does that explain
when I'm coming from Detroit to Flint…

00:46:40.080 --> 00:46:42.840
-…I'm in the suburbs that are really nice.
-Very nice.

00:46:42.907 --> 00:46:47.743
And I see big factories out there
in the suburbs that look really nice.

00:46:47.810 --> 00:46:49.893
-Yes.
-And so they just decentralized?

00:46:49.960 --> 00:46:50.805
Yes, they did.

00:46:50.872 --> 00:46:53.372
They pulled out of the cities because,

00:46:54.144 --> 00:46:57.540
if I'm getting this right,
they saw this as somewhat volatile.

00:46:57.607 --> 00:46:59.614
-I think so.
-Or that's up for argument.

00:46:59.681 --> 00:47:01.081
That's up for debate.

00:47:01.753 --> 00:47:05.497
During the Great Migration,
it wasn't only this city

00:47:05.564 --> 00:47:10.696
that enjoyed the effort and labor
of people from the South.

00:47:10.763 --> 00:47:11.598
Yeah.

00:47:11.665 --> 00:47:15.045
It was all of the manufacturers
including the whole constellation

00:47:15.112 --> 00:47:17.414
of industries
that support the auto industry.

00:47:17.481 --> 00:47:20.698
Well, that's why they all came north,
because the jobs were here.

00:47:20.765 --> 00:47:21.620
And who wouldn't?

00:47:21.687 --> 00:47:24.758
Technology was getting better
in the South with cotton, right?

00:47:24.825 --> 00:47:27.567
Needed less labor and those jobs
paid poorly I believe.

00:47:27.634 --> 00:47:30.617
So why not come up,
work for one of the big three,

00:47:30.684 --> 00:47:33.217
make good money, get health insurance?

00:47:34.000 --> 00:47:37.428
Were they discriminated against? Yes.

00:47:38.622 --> 00:47:39.728
-Yes, they were.
-Okay.

00:47:39.795 --> 00:47:41.907
Where we left, when we crossed that river…

00:47:41.974 --> 00:47:42.807
Yeah.

00:47:43.733 --> 00:47:46.483
People of color
didn't come down to where we're driving.

00:47:46.550 --> 00:47:47.640
-Really?
-No.

00:47:47.707 --> 00:47:50.879
-So I went to the nice part,
which is sort of to the right here,

00:47:50.946 --> 00:47:52.961
and I went up to these nice homes.

00:47:53.028 --> 00:47:55.746
-So it was totally segregated?
-At that time, yes.

00:47:55.813 --> 00:47:57.478
-And when did those times end?

00:47:57.545 --> 00:48:03.256
They didn't really end until really around
the year that I was born, '67 to '72.

00:48:03.653 --> 00:48:04.486
-Okay.

00:48:04.553 --> 00:48:08.228
And I don't think that
people of color felt comfortable

00:48:08.295 --> 00:48:11.467
traveling freely all over this area.

00:48:11.534 --> 00:48:13.649
Okay. So it was really a segregated city.

00:48:13.716 --> 00:48:16.511
And the reason I say that
is because I literally grew up

00:48:16.578 --> 00:48:20.780
the only White kid in
a Black neighborhood not a mile that way.

00:48:20.847 --> 00:48:23.793
-I was the only White kid.
-No way. How was that?

00:48:23.860 --> 00:48:25.327
It was great honestly.

00:48:25.880 --> 00:48:26.713
I was…

00:48:27.913 --> 00:48:31.452
I think I was treated worse
when I hung out with White kids. [laughs]

00:48:31.519 --> 00:48:34.053
I was welcomed.
I was made to feel important.

00:48:34.120 --> 00:48:35.344
I was tolerated.

00:48:35.411 --> 00:48:38.654
They were patient with me,
and I had a good experience.

00:48:38.721 --> 00:48:41.327
-You weren't getting beat up?
-No, didn't happen.

00:48:41.394 --> 00:48:43.773
When I was a little kid, I got…
Yeah, sure.

00:48:43.840 --> 00:48:45.862
I'm a little nerdy kid with glasses.

00:48:45.929 --> 00:48:47.995
I did get bothered.

00:48:48.062 --> 00:48:50.112
But I would have
gotten bothered anywhere.

00:48:50.179 --> 00:48:54.524
Once you come a certain age,
probably around middle school,

00:48:54.591 --> 00:48:56.315
that stuff kind of ends.

00:48:56.382 --> 00:48:58.804
So that ended, right?
And those became my people.

00:48:58.871 --> 00:48:59.996
Those became my friends.

00:49:00.063 --> 00:49:05.434
So I was raised around people
who were born prior to 1900.

00:49:05.501 --> 00:49:09.105
So those folks lived discrimination.

00:49:09.172 --> 00:49:11.030
-They knew no other way.
-Yeah.

00:49:11.097 --> 00:49:15.873
My next door neighbor and another man
basically founded the concept of

00:49:16.460 --> 00:49:19.536
what became
the model for public transportation

00:49:19.603 --> 00:49:21.123
that helped stop that.

00:49:21.190 --> 00:49:25.335
That way, a lady who cleaned houses
on the south side of the river

00:49:25.402 --> 00:49:28.129
didn't have to walk five miles home.

00:49:28.196 --> 00:49:31.077
She could get on a bus,
unmolested, and get home.

00:49:31.144 --> 00:49:32.084
They created that.

00:49:32.627 --> 00:49:34.682
In just a few minutes, two more lights,

00:49:34.755 --> 00:49:39.110
you'll find basically
the birthplace of the modern union.

00:49:39.725 --> 00:49:41.839
-The modern labor union?
-Correct.

00:49:42.187 --> 00:49:43.712
-UAW?
-UAW.

00:49:43.779 --> 00:49:47.567
And your father worked for GM, right?

00:49:47.634 --> 00:49:49.541
-My dad did. Yes.
-Okay.

00:49:50.348 --> 00:49:52.857
My grandfather did
and my great-grandfather did.

00:49:52.924 --> 00:49:53.764
Oh, wow. Okay.

00:49:53.831 --> 00:49:56.968
My great-grandfather
started at Buick in 1906.

00:49:57.035 --> 00:50:02.826
My grandfather came back from World War II
and entered into his career.

00:50:02.893 --> 00:50:03.726
Okay.

00:50:03.793 --> 00:50:08.453
And my father entered in 1964
and he was drafted.

00:50:08.520 --> 00:50:11.351
And something about his time in the Army,

00:50:11.418 --> 00:50:13.820
when he came back from the Army

00:50:14.594 --> 00:50:16.771
he wanted nothing to do
with General Motors.

00:50:16.838 --> 00:50:18.615
And it broke my grandfather's heart

00:50:18.682 --> 00:50:22.010
because my grandfather felt
that in my father that he would realize

00:50:22.077 --> 00:50:24.739
all of the executive dreams
that he didn't realize.

00:50:24.806 --> 00:50:28.936
My dad and my grandfather and
my great-grandfather were management.

00:50:29.003 --> 00:50:32.493
They were the enemies
of the lady we're about to meet.

00:50:32.560 --> 00:50:36.464
But if you look here, this building
right here is the last remnant

00:50:36.531 --> 00:50:40.501
of the offices of a massive facility.

00:50:40.568 --> 00:50:43.884
I went to school
literally next door to this place.

00:50:43.951 --> 00:50:45.379
So this is my middle school.

00:50:45.446 --> 00:50:48.135
-No way.
-This is where I went to middle school.

00:50:48.202 --> 00:50:49.834
-And it's abandoned.

00:50:50.394 --> 00:50:52.006
Oh, that's too bad, huh?

00:50:52.073 --> 00:50:54.097
It's just there's a lack of kids, right?

00:50:54.164 --> 00:50:57.515
I mean, the population
is down more than half.

00:50:57.582 --> 00:50:59.382
-Yep.
-And that's just what happened.

00:50:59.449 --> 00:51:01.012
I wouldn't say it's bad

00:51:01.079 --> 00:51:03.604
'cause that wasn't
the best place to be in the 70s.

00:51:03.671 --> 00:51:06.951
It was a bedlam. it was bedlam.

00:51:08.084 --> 00:51:11.609
There's a tavern here that has
quite a history, The Wooden Keg.

00:51:11.676 --> 00:51:14.104
Many many folks partied here,

00:51:14.171 --> 00:51:16.325
met their wives and husbands here.

00:51:16.392 --> 00:51:17.780
-Right here?
-Yeah.

00:51:17.847 --> 00:51:19.148
-At The Keg?
-Yep.

00:51:19.215 --> 00:51:21.838
-So this is GM currently operating?
-Correct.

00:51:21.905 --> 00:51:25.543
Okay so I thought in Flint all of that
had left but it's still happening?

00:51:25.610 --> 00:51:28.060
-It's still here.
-Okay what are they making here?

00:51:28.127 --> 00:51:29.654
This is mostly truck.

00:51:29.721 --> 00:51:31.073
In fact this truck turning.

00:51:31.140 --> 00:51:34.197
-That's coming out of this.
-Likely came out of that building.

00:51:34.264 --> 00:51:37.224
So there are good
well-paying jobs in town right now.

00:51:37.291 --> 00:51:40.566
-There's a few, sure.
-I mean that's a huge facility.

00:51:41.899 --> 00:51:46.591
So okay, the people working in there,
are most of them living outside of Flint.

00:51:46.658 --> 00:51:48.068
They're not living in Flint?

00:51:48.135 --> 00:51:52.716
I would say probably if you put
a gun to my head and ask me how many.

00:51:52.783 --> 00:51:55.902
-I won't do that.
-I would say probably less than 10%.

00:51:55.969 --> 00:51:58.541
Okay less than 10% live in the city.

00:51:58.614 --> 00:52:01.783
-So GM still has a huge footprint?
-Very much so.

00:52:01.850 --> 00:52:04.814
Maybe it's like the Mon Valley
south of Pittsburgh.

00:52:04.881 --> 00:52:08.531
-I know exactly what you mean.
-Some of those steel jobs are still there.

00:52:08.607 --> 00:52:12.563
But they lives in the country side.
They don't live in the town anymore.

00:52:13.091 --> 00:52:16.793
[Peter] I've seen Tonya Burns everywhere.
Does she have a chance?

00:52:16.860 --> 00:52:18.633
-I'll let Jans answer that question.

00:52:18.700 --> 00:52:24.841
Unfortunately I will say
our political system in this city is…

00:52:25.431 --> 00:52:27.992
it's not, I'm not gonna say corrupt.

00:52:28.059 --> 00:52:29.937
But what I'm gonna say is comical.

00:52:30.004 --> 00:52:34.903
If you know anything about Eric Mays
or any of these guys? Oh man.

00:52:34.970 --> 00:52:37.587
The city council
can't even almost have a meeting

00:52:37.654 --> 00:52:40.515
without the police
arresting council members.

00:52:40.582 --> 00:52:42.576
-Oh geez.
-That's all I'll say.

00:52:42.643 --> 00:52:45.776
-So there's definitely some corruption.
-Oh, it's…

00:52:46.407 --> 00:52:49.743
Okay, Peter,
this is what I'm most excited about.

00:52:50.239 --> 00:52:51.233
There we go.

00:52:51.300 --> 00:52:54.533
It means a lot to me specifically,
I will say.

00:52:54.600 --> 00:52:57.226
My mother's grandfather,
my great-grandfather,

00:52:57.293 --> 00:52:59.260
was an original sit-down striker.

00:52:59.327 --> 00:53:04.160
Okay, so for those that don't know about
labor unions, UAW, sit-down strikers…

00:53:04.227 --> 00:53:05.060
Sure.

00:53:05.127 --> 00:53:08.777
-Union formed because workers felt like
they weren't getting enough, right?

00:53:08.844 --> 00:53:12.118
One of the things that I can tell you,
my great-grandfather

00:53:12.185 --> 00:53:15.216
reiterated to my grandmother
that she said in my childhood,

00:53:15.283 --> 00:53:19.366
I remember specifically
was in a 12-hour shift,

00:53:19.433 --> 00:53:23.723
pre-union, if you even
went to the bathroom, your job was gone.

00:53:23.790 --> 00:53:27.039
-Whoa.
-So they wanted to go to the bathroom.

00:53:27.106 --> 00:53:28.803
Other than that, yeah, right?

00:53:28.870 --> 00:53:33.943
Benefits, they wanted the same thing
they saw the people in white-collar…

00:53:34.010 --> 00:53:35.702
-When did that happen, 1937?

00:53:35.769 --> 00:53:40.766
1937 is when it originally happened, yes,
and it lasted for 44 days.

00:53:41.314 --> 00:53:45.660
During these 44 days,
the plant Fisher, that this took place at,

00:53:45.727 --> 00:53:49.768
they cut the heat off, and it was
middle of winter, the heat was cut off.

00:53:49.835 --> 00:53:54.468
They dealt with the Flint Police,
the state police, National Guard.

00:53:54.535 --> 00:53:57.985
So the employees said we're not working
until you give us more rights.

00:53:58.052 --> 00:53:58.903
Absolutely.

00:53:58.970 --> 00:54:03.157
We're not leaving your factory
until you give us some sort of

00:54:03.860 --> 00:54:05.473
worth of life, right?

00:54:05.540 --> 00:54:08.302
These men had children
that they had to take care of.

00:54:08.369 --> 00:54:09.843
This to me means everything.

00:54:09.910 --> 00:54:12.714
My great-grandmother
died at 98 years old

00:54:12.781 --> 00:54:15.542
and she remembered taking
my great-grandfather food.

00:54:15.609 --> 00:54:18.918
Now this is depiction of the wives

00:54:18.985 --> 00:54:22.876
breaking the windows to throw
their husbands food and clothes.

00:54:22.943 --> 00:54:23.776
-Wow.

00:54:23.843 --> 00:54:27.136
-If you look at this,
this is a depiction of the police

00:54:27.203 --> 00:54:28.995
or authority figure in general.

00:54:29.062 --> 00:54:33.380
I'm not going to name a name but
dragging the wives away from the windows

00:54:33.447 --> 00:54:35.249
from feeding their husbands.

00:54:35.316 --> 00:54:38.943
They had been in there
for 44 days, cold, hungry.

00:54:39.010 --> 00:54:41.174
And if you go here and you look at this,

00:54:41.241 --> 00:54:44.898
it's a small child
that was picketing while their father

00:54:44.965 --> 00:54:46.501
was inside the plant.

00:54:46.568 --> 00:54:49.505
Just fighting for
a chance to take care of them.

00:54:49.572 --> 00:54:51.726
-One thing I read
that I found fascinating.

00:54:51.793 --> 00:54:54.112
So we're talking after the depression.

00:54:54.179 --> 00:54:56.733
Things are still rough
economically, right, In '37.

00:54:56.800 --> 00:54:58.062
-Sure.
-Pre-World War II.

00:54:58.129 --> 00:55:00.086
-Before things really ripped off.
-Yeah.

00:55:00.153 --> 00:55:05.090
So Billy Durant
went from founding GM, right?

00:55:05.619 --> 00:55:09.752
To spending his remaining year…
He went broke. $3 million gone.

00:55:10.370 --> 00:55:14.259
He went broke trying to stop
the 1929 stock market crash.

00:55:14.326 --> 00:55:19.126
He single-handedly tried to buy stock
and keep the market liquid.

00:55:19.193 --> 00:55:21.726
And he tried to convince
other people to do it too.

00:55:21.793 --> 00:55:24.135
Who absolutely thought he was a fool.

00:55:24.202 --> 00:55:28.193
-So yeah, then he went broke
and he ended up his later years,

00:55:28.260 --> 00:55:33.368
running a bowling alley
outside of town here in this timeline.

00:55:33.435 --> 00:55:35.633
You have depression. Things go down.

00:55:35.700 --> 00:55:36.533
Sure.

00:55:36.600 --> 00:55:39.454
He's trying to
get people to buy stock, bring it back.

00:55:39.521 --> 00:55:40.513
He goes broke.

00:55:41.143 --> 00:55:42.703
-This happens.
-Yep.

00:55:42.770 --> 00:55:45.952
And then the war kicks off
and this thing just goes to the moon.

00:55:46.019 --> 00:55:47.413
-Yes.
-Yep.

00:55:47.480 --> 00:55:49.872
It wasn't just General Motors,
Ford, or Chrysler.

00:55:49.939 --> 00:55:51.046
It was the whole nation.

00:55:51.113 --> 00:55:54.287
We fought and won the European Civil War.

00:55:54.758 --> 00:55:57.023
We had…
think about it for a second, guys.

00:55:57.090 --> 00:55:59.109
Europe didn't win the Civil War.

00:55:59.176 --> 00:56:02.415
The World War I and II culmination,
the Americans did.

00:56:02.482 --> 00:56:04.438
And the Soviets too, right?

00:56:04.505 --> 00:56:06.324
And what happened at the end of the war?

00:56:06.391 --> 00:56:09.903
The United States had the most clothes,

00:56:09.970 --> 00:56:13.237
the most food,
by far the most cash, and the bomb.

00:56:13.965 --> 00:56:15.865
-We just had…
-What a time to be alive.

00:56:15.932 --> 00:56:18.215
We had the advantage of
not being destroyed like Europe.

00:56:18.282 --> 00:56:19.896
-Peter…
-What a time to be alive.

00:56:19.963 --> 00:56:22.855
Can I show you this?
This is one thing that…

00:56:22.922 --> 00:56:25.990
And you know, my wife,
we've been together for 15 years,

00:56:26.057 --> 00:56:29.553
but she's convinced
we have such a strong connection.

00:56:29.620 --> 00:56:31.369
These bricks inside this rope,

00:56:31.436 --> 00:56:34.748
they are the names of
all the original sit-down strikers.

00:56:34.815 --> 00:56:38.474
The men who lasted
all 44 days within the plant.

00:56:38.541 --> 00:56:40.002
Now I want to point this out.

00:56:40.069 --> 00:56:44.366
Over here, Bert Christenson
is my great-grandfather, okay?

00:56:45.086 --> 00:56:47.521
Now if we go to the right of this sign,

00:56:48.070 --> 00:56:49.802
the name Reese Easter,

00:56:50.898 --> 00:56:53.298
that is my wife's great-grandfather.

00:56:53.889 --> 00:56:57.213
So my great-grandfather,
my wife's great-grandfather

00:56:57.280 --> 00:56:59.763
spent almost 100 years ago,

00:57:00.441 --> 00:57:04.463
44 days together in a plant
fighting for their rights.

00:57:04.530 --> 00:57:07.258
So we have deep ties here,
deep connections too.

00:57:07.325 --> 00:57:09.487
-So this means a lot to you?
-To me personally.

00:57:09.554 --> 00:57:11.804
I don't know how much
it means to my dad, but…

00:57:11.871 --> 00:57:13.439
This is sacred ground for me.

00:57:13.506 --> 00:57:16.498
If you think of it, a lot of the things
that we enjoy,

00:57:17.151 --> 00:57:19.506
weekends, holidays off.

00:57:20.200 --> 00:57:23.143
-So that's UAW, eight-hour work days?
-Yep.

00:57:23.210 --> 00:57:26.003
This here is my grandmother. Carla.

00:57:26.070 --> 00:57:28.023
Carla, that's who we're going to meet.

00:57:28.090 --> 00:57:31.355
-No way, we're getting to meet her?
-Yeah, this is my grandmother.

00:57:31.422 --> 00:57:34.293
She's still alive.
Her father was an original striker.

00:57:34.360 --> 00:57:35.193
She retired.

00:57:35.260 --> 00:57:37.232
-How old is she?
-She's in her eighties.

00:57:37.299 --> 00:57:40.353
-Okay, where is GM now?
Is UAW still fully involved?

00:57:40.420 --> 00:57:41.223
Oh yes.

00:57:41.290 --> 00:57:43.251
-That car right there?
-Yeah.

00:57:43.318 --> 00:57:45.360
If the union guys were here,
would have it towed.

00:57:45.427 --> 00:57:47.553
That car should not be
in that parking lot.

00:57:47.620 --> 00:57:51.753
-Oh, so to this day if someone has
a Japanese car, Asian car,

00:57:51.820 --> 00:57:52.845
you're not popular?

00:57:52.912 --> 00:57:54.034
Not at all.

00:57:54.101 --> 00:57:56.245
Thank God they don't have a security guard

00:57:56.312 --> 00:57:58.812
because them dudes
would kick us off this property.

00:57:58.880 --> 00:58:02.817
-You were saying you didn't buy
a Buick for what reason? Why?

00:58:02.884 --> 00:58:04.329
They fired my best friend.

00:58:04.396 --> 00:58:05.925
Fired my best friend flat out.

00:58:05.992 --> 00:58:08.977
And he was one of the smartest people
I've ever known in my whole life.

00:58:09.044 --> 00:58:11.824
It's a shame that they let him go
because he helped them.

00:58:11.891 --> 00:58:13.992
He was a benefit there.
He didn't cost them.

00:58:14.059 --> 00:58:16.163
He was helping them. It's a shame.

00:58:16.230 --> 00:58:18.293
-So just out of principle, you're out?

00:58:18.360 --> 00:58:20.181
-I'm done.
-He thinks it's crazy.

00:58:20.248 --> 00:58:21.223
He has two GM cars.

00:58:21.290 --> 00:58:25.282
He doesn't have the same affliction
that I have, but I'm mad as hell.

00:58:25.349 --> 00:58:26.282
So here we go.

00:58:29.502 --> 00:58:32.843
[Peter] Okay, so the union stuff,
I don't know enough about it.

00:58:32.910 --> 00:58:35.516
It makes sense from the outside.

00:58:35.583 --> 00:58:37.562
Who doesn't want more worker rights?

00:58:37.629 --> 00:58:40.326
I'm sure there's
a good argument against it.

00:58:40.393 --> 00:58:42.547
And the one I was thinking of is,

00:58:42.614 --> 00:58:45.209
look, the countries
that don't play by those rules,

00:58:45.276 --> 00:58:50.395
if we're not protecting our industry,
then they can destroy us on price.

00:58:50.462 --> 00:58:54.594
Like if Chinese cars tomorrow could come
to America we would kill a lot of our…

00:58:54.661 --> 00:58:56.668
Not unless we reverse tariff, right?

00:58:56.735 --> 00:59:00.435
In other words for every car that
General Motors or Ford or Chrysler builds,

00:59:00.502 --> 00:59:03.780
the United States government
gives them X amount of dollars,

00:59:03.860 --> 00:59:06.851
X percentage of the value
of the content of that vehicle.

00:59:06.918 --> 00:59:07.751
Yeah.

00:59:07.818 --> 00:59:10.433
We tried that in 2008. It was not popular.

00:59:10.901 --> 00:59:14.779
Okay. But say from my recollection,
I could be off on that.

00:59:14.846 --> 00:59:19.106
Germany taking our cars in,
they charged a 12% tariff.

00:59:19.173 --> 00:59:24.563
Us taking cars from Germany
until recently, it was a 2% tariff.

00:59:24.630 --> 00:59:26.855
-It wasn't even.
-It wasn't even.

00:59:26.922 --> 00:59:30.358
So they get
an advantage in a sense, right?

00:59:30.804 --> 00:59:32.093
I would think so.

00:59:32.160 --> 00:59:35.483
I know that's changed. I don't know
where tariffs are at right now.

00:59:35.550 --> 00:59:38.060
It's impossible to follow it seems like.

00:59:38.127 --> 00:59:41.302
-It's desperately confusing to me.
-It's desperately confusing.

00:59:41.376 --> 00:59:42.378
-I have no clue.
-No.

00:59:42.464 --> 00:59:46.895
But reciprocal, as in Germany
pays 12% for our vehicles,

00:59:46.962 --> 00:59:48.905
we pay 12% for their vehicles.

00:59:48.972 --> 00:59:50.446
that makes sense to me.

00:59:50.513 --> 00:59:52.016
-Right? That's fair.
-I think…

00:59:52.083 --> 00:59:55.216
No tariff in either direction
makes sense to me.

00:59:56.350 --> 01:00:00.980
But look, if you have
the Chinese auto market being subsidized,

01:00:01.047 --> 01:00:03.900
let's just say the government's
going to put money into it

01:00:03.967 --> 01:00:08.287
'cause they're really good at business
and understanding how to win the game.

01:00:08.354 --> 01:00:11.231
And so they realize
we want to get into the US car market.

01:00:11.298 --> 01:00:13.851
We're gonna actually
subsidize whatever company.

01:00:13.918 --> 01:00:18.467
We're gonna get them in,
US has no tariffs on the Chinese car.

01:00:19.095 --> 01:00:21.666
Then the Chinese car,
because the quality's better,

01:00:21.733 --> 01:00:23.213
the technology's there,

01:00:23.280 --> 01:00:26.921
is going to decimate our auto industry.

01:00:26.988 --> 01:00:29.498
So you need to have some sort of
protection, right?

01:00:29.565 --> 01:00:31.746
Because their labor's cheaper,

01:00:31.813 --> 01:00:33.548
the labor laws are different.

01:00:34.082 --> 01:00:36.032
Like, make that make sense to me, Dean.

01:00:36.099 --> 01:00:42.407
The Chinese vehicle, the ones that we see
that are so modern and so well-equipped,

01:00:42.474 --> 01:00:48.311
so beautiful, and what, the top-level
Tesla compared to a BYD whatever,

01:00:48.378 --> 01:00:51.632
there's a $60,000, $80,000 price
discrepancy,

01:00:51.699 --> 01:00:55.067
it's because that vehicle
was built for that market.

01:00:55.134 --> 01:00:58.004
I don't necessarily believe
that that same vehicle,

01:00:58.071 --> 01:01:01.649
if you just uprooted it
and plopped it here in Flint, Michigan,

01:01:01.717 --> 01:01:03.013
would fit this market.

01:01:03.080 --> 01:01:04.674
I don't think that it's a fair…

01:01:04.741 --> 01:01:07.578
-Yeah, but they would adjust it,
just like Nissan, right?

01:01:07.651 --> 01:01:10.201
They would have to
because there's safety standards.

01:01:10.268 --> 01:01:13.250
Yeah, but look at Nissan, or Toyota.

01:01:13.317 --> 01:01:16.751
They don't have monster
pickup trucks in Japan, they adjust.

01:01:16.818 --> 01:01:20.451
They figure out how to do it here
and they put their plants here.

01:01:20.518 --> 01:01:21.365
Yes.

01:01:21.432 --> 01:01:23.497
So, anyways, this is a long conversation.

01:01:23.564 --> 01:01:25.282
No, no, no, it's fine.

01:01:25.349 --> 01:01:30.025
I think that what you're getting at is
an equitable balance in trade.

01:01:30.092 --> 01:01:30.925
Yeah.

01:01:30.992 --> 01:01:34.810
And if we are operating at an imbalance
then that has to be corrected.

01:01:34.877 --> 01:01:37.438
-Right, or these jobs go away.
-Yes.

01:01:38.024 --> 01:01:39.611
[Peter] And then we get more Flints.

01:01:39.678 --> 01:01:43.339
[Dean] The reality of the matter is
yes, it can happen here too

01:01:43.406 --> 01:01:46.033
in your community,
in your city, in your town.

01:01:46.100 --> 01:01:50.312
If I were to stand
on the corner of one of the streets

01:01:50.379 --> 01:01:52.021
that we saw that looked terrible,

01:01:52.088 --> 01:01:57.183
and said that in X number of years this is
all going to be gone, burned, blown up,

01:01:58.058 --> 01:02:01.883
rotted, no one would have believed me.
They would have said I was crazy.

01:02:01.950 --> 01:02:06.327
I don't want the key takeaway to… 
Dean's saying that basically this is what…

01:02:06.394 --> 01:02:08.694
Everything is gonna
go to hell in a handbasket.

01:02:08.761 --> 01:02:13.639
Honestly, how many McDonald's jobs
are going to support a society?

01:02:14.122 --> 01:02:16.056
-Yeah.
-Not a whole lot, right?

01:02:16.123 --> 01:02:19.428
No offense, and I'm not knocking
anybody that works for McDonald's,

01:02:19.495 --> 01:02:20.952
but it's not a future.

01:02:21.019 --> 01:02:23.007
So we need skilled jobs.

01:02:23.074 --> 01:02:25.175
We need living wages.

01:02:25.242 --> 01:02:26.779
We need health care.

01:02:26.846 --> 01:02:29.513
-We need childcare.
-So back to my point.

01:02:30.156 --> 01:02:33.051
Then you need
those industries to have a chance

01:02:33.118 --> 01:02:37.102
and if they get decimated
through global competition…

01:02:37.169 --> 01:02:38.002
Correct.

01:02:38.069 --> 01:02:40.673
Then you're gonna lose those industries
and you get more Flints.

01:02:40.740 --> 01:02:41.748
Yes.

01:02:41.815 --> 01:02:46.256
So that just makes logic to me,
and I don't understand it at a deep level.

01:02:46.323 --> 01:02:49.342
-'Cause I haven't put enough time in it.
-Me either, honestly.

01:02:49.409 --> 01:02:50.702
-Okay.
-I agree.

01:02:50.769 --> 01:02:51.850
I'm with you, right?

01:02:51.917 --> 01:02:54.726
And I don't want to sit here and say
that I know what the answers are.

01:02:54.793 --> 01:02:55.817
-I do not.
-Yeah.

01:02:55.884 --> 01:02:59.212
I can tell you
what the aftermath looks like.

01:02:59.279 --> 01:03:00.212
Yeah, you can.

01:03:01.030 --> 01:03:02.814
And it's not nice, huh?

01:03:02.881 --> 01:03:04.013
Unfortunately, yes.

01:03:04.571 --> 01:03:07.113
-We're outside of Flint proper right now?
-Correct.

01:03:07.180 --> 01:03:09.761
It just totally changes
once you get out of the border.

01:03:09.828 --> 01:03:11.277
-It's a hard line.
-You see it.

01:03:11.344 --> 01:03:12.393
You see it and feel it.

01:03:12.460 --> 01:03:14.975
-This is where Grandma lives?
-Yes, sir.

01:03:15.042 --> 01:03:16.997
-And this is a couple miles from Flint?

01:03:17.064 --> 01:03:19.859
-Yeah, maybe to my house
is probably two miles.

01:03:19.926 --> 01:03:20.893
-Okay.

01:03:22.954 --> 01:03:25.128
Grandma, this is Peter.
Peter, this is my grandma.

01:03:25.195 --> 01:03:28.140
How you doing? Nice to meet you.

01:03:28.700 --> 01:03:32.004
So Carla, we just saw your brick
out there at the memorial.

01:03:32.071 --> 01:03:34.853
Oh, yeah.
It's been there for quite a while.

01:03:34.920 --> 01:03:36.789
And Jens was saying you have stories.

01:03:36.855 --> 01:03:39.593
-This here.
You want me to tell it, Grandma?

01:03:39.660 --> 01:03:43.504
This here was a nail
that he would take a potato

01:03:43.571 --> 01:03:48.153
and drive the nail into the potato
and put in the oven.

01:03:48.220 --> 01:03:52.446
And the idea was so that the heat would
distribute evenly through the potato.

01:03:52.513 --> 01:03:56.818
They had fires in the plant
during the sit-down.

01:03:56.885 --> 01:03:59.099
They had to. It was wintertime.

01:03:59.166 --> 01:04:01.396
-Oh, they were making fire in there?
-Yeah.

01:04:01.463 --> 01:04:03.563
[Jens] Yeah. They cut off the heat.

01:04:03.630 --> 01:04:06.024
-Cut off the natural gas.
-Cut off everything.

01:04:06.091 --> 01:04:08.801
-So these were for…
this is just for food in there?

01:04:08.868 --> 01:04:11.068
-Uh-huh.
-To warm the potatoes up?

01:04:11.607 --> 01:04:12.576
Oh, wow.

01:04:13.799 --> 01:04:14.915
What do you got there?

01:04:14.988 --> 01:04:17.959
This is something they did

01:04:18.867 --> 01:04:22.249
after Walter Ruther
died in the plane crash.

01:04:22.316 --> 01:04:23.163
-Who's this?

01:04:23.230 --> 01:04:26.293
He was the president
when the whole thing started.

01:04:26.360 --> 01:04:28.901
-The UAW president? Okay.
-Uh-huh.

01:04:28.968 --> 01:04:32.194
So your mother was getting food
through the window.

01:04:32.261 --> 01:04:35.383
There was a lot of women
down there tossing food up

01:04:35.450 --> 01:04:38.450
and getting clubbed by the police.

01:04:38.990 --> 01:04:41.423
It was like a riot. It was a riot.

01:04:42.180 --> 01:04:44.291
-So you grew up in Flint, obviously.
-Uh-huh.

01:04:44.358 --> 01:04:45.425
-Did you love it?

01:04:45.983 --> 01:04:48.318
Yeah, it was great back then.

01:04:49.065 --> 01:04:50.489
Not so hot now.

01:04:51.037 --> 01:04:55.091
So back in the day in Flint,
that theater, Capitol Theater, was busy.

01:04:55.158 --> 01:04:57.823
-People walking the streets.
-Oh, yeah.

01:04:57.882 --> 01:05:00.651
-I'm sure people dressed well, right?
-Mm-hmm.

01:05:01.195 --> 01:05:03.318
Nice stores downtown.

01:05:03.385 --> 01:05:04.882
-Nice stores downtown.

01:05:05.393 --> 01:05:07.997
And since it was relatively a new city,

01:05:08.064 --> 01:05:10.564
it would have been very clean
and sparkling, right?

01:05:10.631 --> 01:05:11.773
Yeah, it was nice.

01:05:12.236 --> 01:05:14.455
Beautiful place to grow up.

01:05:15.555 --> 01:05:17.659
Real good schools.

01:05:17.726 --> 01:05:19.913
[Jens] Irony there, right? To see.

01:05:19.980 --> 01:05:25.285
'Cause we passed by the school
that was his that's dilapidated now.

01:05:25.720 --> 01:05:28.731
And just one generation removed from that,

01:05:29.578 --> 01:05:32.718
she's telling you that her schools
were amazing, right?

01:05:32.785 --> 01:05:35.904
-You see what I mean?
-Right. And now how is it for yours?

01:05:35.971 --> 01:05:39.388
Well, my kids can't even go to 'em.
They're just gone.

01:05:39.455 --> 01:05:44.371
-Where do your kids go to school?
-A school in the next town over.

01:05:44.438 --> 01:05:46.193
So right now we're in Grand Blanc.

01:05:46.260 --> 01:05:49.346
And between Flint
and Grand Blanc is Burton.

01:05:49.413 --> 01:05:50.413
-Okay.

01:05:50.480 --> 01:05:53.568
Do you wish you grew up
at a different time in Flint history?

01:05:53.635 --> 01:05:57.521
Uh, you know,
Flint was rough for me for sure.

01:05:57.588 --> 01:06:00.211
-So you got it at its peak.
-Yeah.

01:06:00.278 --> 01:06:03.016
Dean saw it at
the end of the peak in the decline.

01:06:03.083 --> 01:06:05.693
And you just came in
to this world at the bottom.

01:06:05.760 --> 01:06:07.756
The roughest part of time.

01:06:07.823 --> 01:06:12.812
Okay. And then what's in your kid's
rough also? And we'll see where that goes.

01:06:12.879 --> 01:06:16.475
So the problem
that I struggle with, parentally, right?

01:06:16.542 --> 01:06:19.440
That's the biggest thing is
there's nothing here, right?

01:06:19.507 --> 01:06:21.706
So every day that I spend here…

01:06:22.225 --> 01:06:23.446
-In Flint?
-In Flint.

01:06:23.513 --> 01:06:26.700
I feel like
I'm doing my own children a disservice

01:06:26.767 --> 01:06:32.591
because the fact of the matter is, yes,
there's 5,000 GM jobs left in Flint,

01:06:32.658 --> 01:06:34.856
but there's not much of anything else.

01:06:34.923 --> 01:06:37.737
That just tells me automatically
my children will have to

01:06:37.804 --> 01:06:41.326
venture out into the country
to find opportunity,

01:06:41.393 --> 01:06:44.408
which puts them
farther away from me, right?

01:06:44.953 --> 01:06:49.048
It doesn't keep them within town,
finding good jobs and benefits in town.

01:06:49.115 --> 01:06:51.544
My grandkids aren't gonna be in town.

01:06:51.611 --> 01:06:55.699
I'm highly aware of this
and that's one thing that I struggle with.

01:06:55.766 --> 01:07:01.156
It's likely, to be honest,
it's probably within my three-year plan

01:07:01.223 --> 01:07:04.209
to remove myself and children from Flint.

01:07:04.750 --> 01:07:07.100
I don't have any other choice anymore,
you know?

01:07:07.167 --> 01:07:08.682
I'll tell you how safe it was.

01:07:08.749 --> 01:07:11.378
My brother and I, my brother, Peter,

01:07:12.312 --> 01:07:16.592
we decided one day
to run away just for the day.

01:07:17.200 --> 01:07:21.008
And we went a couple miles
up past Ballenger Highway.

01:07:21.075 --> 01:07:23.142
And there was nothing up there.

01:07:24.121 --> 01:07:29.054
We got back on our bikes about 5:00
and thought, oh, we're going to get it.

01:07:30.410 --> 01:07:34.039
We got home and my folks
had been canning tomatoes all day

01:07:34.106 --> 01:07:36.373
and they didn't know we were gone.

01:07:37.350 --> 01:07:39.724
[Jens] Yeah, and you know,
talk about wages.

01:07:39.791 --> 01:07:43.916
You mentioned it earlier
and my great-grandfather, her father,

01:07:43.983 --> 01:07:45.196
he raised nine children.

01:07:45.263 --> 01:07:47.563
My great-grandmother
was a stay-at-home mother.

01:07:47.630 --> 01:07:49.233
They took vacations.

01:07:49.300 --> 01:07:51.632
They had shoes.
They had… you know what I mean?

01:07:51.699 --> 01:07:55.333
So he was able to provide
a life for nine children.

01:07:55.400 --> 01:07:57.374
-How many children did you have?
-Four.

01:07:57.441 --> 01:07:59.535
-Four. And did you stay home with them?

01:07:59.602 --> 01:08:00.524
No.

01:08:00.591 --> 01:08:03.993
I worked 30 years at AC Spark Club.

01:08:04.060 --> 01:08:07.195
-Okay, when they were young,
you were working at AC Spark Plug.

01:08:07.262 --> 01:08:08.113
Mm-hmm.

01:08:08.180 --> 01:08:13.925
That's just something
they gave out on White Shirt Day.

01:08:14.538 --> 01:08:15.823
-What's White Shirt Day?

01:08:15.890 --> 01:08:18.516
-Something my mother and dad
came up with.

01:08:18.583 --> 01:08:20.312
-Sit Down Strike Watch.

01:08:20.867 --> 01:08:24.768
-That's the 75th anniversary UAW Watch.
-Okay, I couldn't read it.

01:08:24.835 --> 01:08:27.333
Yeah, the White Shirt Day, that came up.

01:08:27.400 --> 01:08:30.202
My mother and dad
were sitting there one night

01:08:30.740 --> 01:08:33.868
trying to think of
what they could do to remember

01:08:35.000 --> 01:08:36.933
all that they'd been through.

01:08:38.180 --> 01:08:41.771
They came up with white shirt
because supervision,

01:08:41.838 --> 01:08:48.480
they had to wear white because they were
so much better than the hourly people.

01:08:49.787 --> 01:08:51.121
And my dad said,

01:08:52.414 --> 01:08:56.617
we could all wear white and we'd be
just as good as they are. So they do.

01:08:56.684 --> 01:09:00.036
I think it's
the 11th of February every year.

01:09:00.710 --> 01:09:04.043
-So UAW still to this day
is very important for you?

01:09:04.597 --> 01:09:07.394
Well, yeah, I get my pension. [laughs]

01:09:08.631 --> 01:09:10.631
-But I heard you drive a Honda.

01:09:11.287 --> 01:09:13.247
-Yeah.
-Tell us about that.

01:09:14.878 --> 01:09:17.109
[Dean] There's more
union content in that Honda

01:09:17.176 --> 01:09:19.531
than you'll find in a Chevrolet whatever.

01:09:19.598 --> 01:09:20.798
-You serious?
-Yes.

01:09:21.490 --> 01:09:23.340
And they're built here in the US too.

01:09:23.407 --> 01:09:25.140
Yeah, they are down South.

01:09:26.019 --> 01:09:30.059
We had union meetings
in the living room of our house.

01:09:31.182 --> 01:09:35.014
For several years I think.

01:09:35.081 --> 01:09:38.056
-Did you have any idea
how big this would become?

01:09:38.123 --> 01:09:41.545
When you're having a union meeting
in your house and then the UAW is…

01:09:41.612 --> 01:09:45.440
No, it didn't affect us kids.
We were too young.

01:09:45.507 --> 01:09:46.340
-Oh, yeah.

01:09:46.407 --> 01:09:50.131
I remember cleaning house and my
mother cooking,

01:09:50.198 --> 01:09:52.485
making sandwiches for everybody.

01:09:53.133 --> 01:09:56.157
-So, in a way, they were activists.
-Pretty much.

01:09:56.224 --> 01:09:58.593
-Sorry, this is my Aunt Andrea.
-How you doing?

01:09:58.660 --> 01:10:02.638
-She's retired General Motors as well.
-Oh, nice. The full crew in here.

01:10:02.705 --> 01:10:03.756
Yeah.

01:10:03.823 --> 01:10:05.523
-You gonna go back to work?

01:10:05.590 --> 01:10:07.155
You gonna get out of retirement?

01:10:07.222 --> 01:10:08.373
Oh, they put me out.

01:10:08.440 --> 01:10:10.710
-They put you out?
-They put me out.

01:10:10.777 --> 01:10:14.173
No, no, I can collect Social Security.
I'm not going back.

01:10:16.167 --> 01:10:17.543
It's not like it was.

01:10:17.610 --> 01:10:20.258
Her, her husband,
both retired General Motors.

01:10:20.325 --> 01:10:22.313
-It's not like it was. How so?

01:10:22.380 --> 01:10:24.068
Well, only from what I hear

01:10:24.135 --> 01:10:26.463
because I haven't
been in there in a long time.

01:10:26.530 --> 01:10:29.963
But everybody's got headphones on now
and they're doing their own thing.

01:10:30.030 --> 01:10:33.363
We used to have fun in there.
You joke, you laugh, you…

01:10:33.430 --> 01:10:35.412
This is a survivor series.

01:10:35.479 --> 01:10:37.776
-You should tell them about…
-I have to say…

01:10:37.843 --> 01:10:38.676
Go, Dean.

01:10:39.600 --> 01:10:44.238
So, what you see here
are people who went into these workplaces.

01:10:44.924 --> 01:10:48.180
And did literally
mind-numbing, very difficult work,

01:10:48.247 --> 01:10:50.342
repetitive work, difficult work.

01:10:50.409 --> 01:10:52.986
And they didn't lose
their soul in the process.

01:10:53.053 --> 01:10:56.409
A lot of folks,
they came out alcoholics, drug addicts.

01:10:56.476 --> 01:10:58.951
They maybe didn't come out at all.
These people did.

01:10:59.018 --> 01:11:02.155
And they came out
with their spirit and heart intact.

01:11:02.706 --> 01:11:04.099
That's not valueless.

01:11:04.166 --> 01:11:07.040
That's massive to me personally.

01:11:20.169 --> 01:11:23.195
All right, guys.
End of the road today. Flint, Michigan.

01:11:23.262 --> 01:11:25.198
Quite an interesting place.

01:11:25.265 --> 01:11:28.920
And there's so much
that we didn't get into.

01:11:29.480 --> 01:11:31.983
But I want to leave you with a few things.

01:11:32.050 --> 01:11:34.781
When a company like GM that pretty much

01:11:35.610 --> 01:11:40.260
employs everyone in town
leaves a place like Flint,

01:11:40.327 --> 01:11:45.385
the cycle of just a
hard, hard, hard landing happens.

01:11:45.452 --> 01:11:47.914
I've seen it all across the country.

01:11:47.981 --> 01:11:51.553
And the good thing I would say
from the people we talked to today

01:11:51.620 --> 01:11:53.587
is it's reached its bottom.

01:11:54.252 --> 01:11:57.693
It might be coming off
that bottom a bit and going up.

01:11:57.760 --> 01:12:02.309
It will be interesting to see this place
in 10, 15 years and where it goes.

01:12:02.376 --> 01:12:06.819
Maybe it follows Downtown Detroit's story,
which is quite impressive.

01:12:06.886 --> 01:12:08.172
Hopefully it does.

01:12:08.239 --> 01:12:11.780
And then lastly,
go check out John Hammon.

01:12:12.847 --> 01:12:14.767
Yeah, those guys seem so cool.

01:12:14.834 --> 01:12:17.278
And I bet the music scene is awesome here.

01:12:17.345 --> 01:12:20.675
The good thing that comes
through hard times and strife

01:12:20.742 --> 01:12:23.510
is quite often the creatives come out.

01:12:24.046 --> 01:12:26.610
And they get to painting
and making beautiful murals

01:12:26.677 --> 01:12:29.209
and making awesome music to listen to.

01:12:29.276 --> 01:12:31.757
Thank you
for coming along on this journey.

01:12:31.824 --> 01:12:33.091
Until the next one.

01:12:33.158 --> 01:12:35.710
[soulful music]

